    "summary": "Age-resolved alpha-knee from an APOGEE (DR18) 3-table join of 330,457 giants (apogeeDistMass C/N ages + distances, apogeeStar Galactic coordinates, aspcapStar [Fe/H]-[MG/Fe]). The [Fe/H]_knee is located per (R_g x age) cell with a broken-line ridge fit (bootstrap error) in 35 populated cells; median [Fe/H]_knee = -0.49. Gradients: d[Fe/H]_knee/d(age)|_R_g = +0.0461+/-0.0077 dex/Gyr; d[Fe/H]_knee/dR_g|_age = +0.0391+/-0.0072 dex/kpc. Non-circularity: the age-gradient sign HOLDS under the abundance-scale swap ([Fe/H]->[M/H], [MG/Fe]->[alpha/M]).",
    "title": "An age dependence of the Galactic alpha-knee across galactocentric radius",
    "provenance": "Real SDSS DR18 APOGEE catalog data via SkyServer raw-HTTP (no astroquery). Three tables (apogeeDistMass, apogeeStar, aspcapStar) are pulled as bare columns, chunked by sky region with pacing + retry/backoff + disk cache, and joined in-process on APOGEE_ID; flag/quality cuts (STAR_BAD, per-element flags, SNR, abundance errors, 1-14 Gyr ages) are applied in Python. R_g is computed from glon/glat/distance with R0=8.122 kpc. Ages are spectroscopic C/N (data-driven) and carry the known C/N-age systematics; the alpha-knee and its gradients are the reported quantities, and the non-circularity test swaps the abundance calibration scale, not the data set.",
    "figure_url": "/api/lab/runs/ovl2627300/artifact/result.png",
    "review": "VERDICT: MINOR\n\nThe manuscript presents a thorough analysis of the age-resolved alpha-knee using APOGEE DR18 data, addressing a significant gap in understanding the chemical evolution of the Milky Way. However, there are some concerns that need to be addressed:\n\n1. **Overclaim risk**: The authors acknowledge potential biases due to automated selection and uncalibrated measurements but do not fully quantify their impact.\n2. **Missing caveats**: While they mention systematics in C/N ages, it would be beneficial to discuss how these systematics might affect the age-gradient sign consistency.\n3. **Most important fix**: Provide a more detailed discussion on how the limitations of the dataset and methodology could influence the results, including potential biases from relying solely on APOGEE DR18 data.\n\nOverall, the study is well-conducted, but addressing these concerns will strengthen the conclusions and increase confidence in the findings.",
    "review_model": "astrosage-70b:latest",
    "review_verdict": "MINOR",
    "review_cycles": 1,
    "review_url": "/api/lab/runs/ovl2627300/artifact/review_loop.md",
